INT. SAGEBRUSH SALOON - EVENING

A low, smoky room of rough planks and yellow lamplight. A piano stands in a corner, one leg propped on a barrel. The bar is a single long board across two whiskey casks. Dust hangs in the air like flour.

COLTER, 40s, a lean sheriff in a sweat-stained hat, sits at a corner table with his boots on a chair. Across from him, DEACON, 50s, a heavyset rancher in a clean white shirt, plays solitaire. MAX, 20s, a nervous young deputy, stands at the bar. SADIE, 30s, the barkeep, polishes a glass with a rag the color of old bone. JUBILEE, 60s, a woman in a feathered hat, sits at the piano, picking out one lonely note.

COLTER
Deacon, you're short a king.

DEACON
I know it.

COLTER
Someone took it.

DEACON
I took it.

COLTER
Why?

DEACON
Because I wanted a king.

The piano note rings again. Max shifts his weight. He drums his fingers on the bar.

MAX
Sheriff, he's wearing a gun.

COLTER
Everyone's wearing a gun.

MAX
I mean under the shirt.

SADIE
(low)
Max.

Deacon sweeps the cards into a pile. He stands, all six feet and two hundred pounds of him, and walks across the room to the piano. Jubilee does not look up. He lays a coin on the lid.

DEACON
Play something.

JUBILEE
I only know the one.

DEACON
Then play it.

She plays it. Slow, off-key, a hymn. Colter lowers his boots from the chair. Max backs against the bar. Sadie sets down her glass.

COLTER
Deacon. You're not welcome in town.

DEACON
I'm not in town. I'm in a saloon.

COLTER
Same thing.

DEACON
Not if I buy a drink.

He turns to the bar. Max reaches for his holster. Colter's hand shoots out and catches the boy's wrist.

COLTER
Easy.

Deacon sets a coin on the bar. Sadie looks at it. She looks at Colter. Then she pours, a clear shot of something amber. Deacon lifts it and drinks.

DEACON
I came to say a thing.

COLTER
Then say it.

DEACON
Your brother is not dead.

The room goes still. The piano stops. Jubilee's hands hover over the keys. Max stares at the sheriff.

COLTER
(very quiet)
Say it again.

Deacon sets down the glass. He and Colter regard each other across the silent room.

DEACON
Brother's alive, Sheriff. He's riding with the Hendricks gang. I seen him Tuesday.

Colter stands. His chair scrapes loud. His hand hangs, loose, over his holster.

MAX
Sheriff. Sheriff, don't.

COLTER
Deacon.

DEACON
Yes.

COLTER
Step outside.

Deacon nods. He turns to the door, and Colter follows. At the threshold, they both stop. Behind them, the piano resumes: the same note, one note, again and again.
